// Pipelined schedule: `step_or_seed` on a dedicated compute worker,
// `ident` staging on host, with the inter-generation `grid` symbol
// carried as an async buffered channel.
//
// Two workers:
//   host     -- runs the effectful I/O kernels (load_input,
//               save_output) and the scalar `ident` staging kernel
//               (the loop that copies grid[ITERS] -> result).
//   compute  -- runs the stencil kernel `step_or_seed`. Each (t, i)
//               application reads three cells of grid[t-1] (modulo
//               the wrap-around index) and the corresponding seed
//               cell, branching on `t` to pick which path; writes
//               one cell of grid[t].
//
// Capability surface exercised (PRD §9 row 11, M4):
//   - `transfer grid : async, buffer=2, notify=event`
//     The async-streaming channel for the inter-generation state.
//     `buffer=2` = double-buffer (the literal "double-buffer" the
//     PRD row names) — at steady state one slot holds generation
//     `t` being written while the other holds generation `t-1`
//     being read. `notify=event` = condvar-style readiness
//     notification (not a barrier-style sync), which only the
//     async backend supports.
//   - `loop t : pipeline=2`
//     Pipeline depth 2 on the outer iteration axis, matched to
//     `buffer=2` so the pipeline-depth-<=-buffer-capacity link
//     gate (TASK-0134, PRD §8.2) is exactly at the bound.
//
// Why two workers, not three
// --------------------------
// Example 9 (producer/consumer pipe) uses three workers (host +
// producer + consumer) because its loop body has two distinct compute
// kernels in a two-stage chain. This example has a single compute
// kernel (`step_or_seed`) iterated over the time dimension, so the
// natural shape is host + one compute worker. The grid array is the
// load-bearing cross-worker symbol: compute writes every cell of
// `grid` inside the iteration loop (each `step_or_seed` call), and
// host reads `grid[ITERS]` (via `ident` in the result-stage loop).
// `seed` is loaded on host and consumed by the compute kernel (via
// the seed-cell argument to each `step_or_seed` call) — also
// cross-worker. The link step's `data_is_cross_worker` check
// (TASK-0214) inspects every kernel touching `grid` —
// `{ident @ host, step_or_seed @ compute}` covers two distinct
// workers, so `grid` is genuinely cross-worker.
//
// Honest scope note: pipelining inside the per-generation loop
// ---------------------------------------------------------
// With `step_or_seed` alone on `compute`, the per-(t, i) body of the
// inner stencil loop is a single-worker chunk. `transfer_inject`'s
// same-worker skip (TASK-0214) means no per-iteration Xfer is emitted
// between consecutive `step_or_seed` applications on `compute` —
// they share a worker, so the grid array is intra-compute inside the
// iteration body. The `loop t : pipeline=2` directive therefore does
// NOT introduce genuine stage-parallelism inside the iteration loop
// on this schedule — its primary effect is to drive the IR-level
// `initial_marking=2` on the `grid` Push/Wait pair (so the link gate
// validates the pipeline-buffer match) and to pin the M4 capability
// surface end-to-end (`async + buffer=2 + notify=event` on the
// cross-worker `grid` symbol — the cross-worker hops happen at the
// boundaries, host -> compute via `seed` and compute -> host via
// the `result`-staging `ident` reads on `grid[ITERS]`). The genuine
// stage-parallelism fixture in this milestone is example 09 (two
// distinct compute kernels on two distinct workers, `pipeline=4`
// matched to `buffer=4`); example 11 is the iterated-state companion
// that exercises the SAME capability vector through the SAME e2e
// gate, on a different algorithmic shape (cross-iteration data
// dependency rather than cross-stage chaining).
//
// Why this schedule cannot run on pthreads-sync / mp-tcp-bufsync
// -------------------------------------------------------------
// Both backends' capabilities.toml declare `supports_async = false`,
// `supports_buffer = false`, `notify = ["polled"|"barrier"]`. The
// `transfer grid` clause here requires all three (async + buffer>1 +
// notify=event), so the capability check rejects this schedule on
// those backends BEFORE codegen. The e2e harness reports the
// corresponding cells as [[skip]] with the cited capability
// mismatch; see e2e-matrix.toml. pthreads-async is the tier-1
// backend whose capability surface satisfies this schedule.
//
// What's intentionally NOT here
// -----------------------------
//   - No `block=`, no `vectorize=`. The pipeline transform is the
//     only loop transform; the inner `i` loop is left alone.
//   - No distributed placement (`place step_or_seed on { c0, c1, ... }`).
//     A single compute worker is the smallest fixture for the
//     pipelined capability surface on a single-stage body.
//   - No `partition=workers` on the inner `i` loop. The grid is
//     small (N=32) and partitioning the spatial axis is example
//     03/05's territory, not the M4 double-buffer surface.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, compute };

    // I/O on host (owns the file system).
    place load_input  on host;
    place save_output on host;

    // Result staging on host (the `ident` loop reads `grid[ITERS]`).
    place ident on host;

    // The stencil step (with seed-fallback for t==0) on the
    // dedicated compute worker. Each application reads three cells
    // of the previous generation (or pre-initialised zeros at t==0)
    // and the corresponding seed cell, and writes one cell of the
    // current generation.
    place step_or_seed on compute;

    // Pipeline depth 2 on the outer iteration axis, matched to
    // `buffer=2` on `grid` below. The link gate (TASK-0134) checks
    // `depth <= buffer` for every data symbol intersected in
    // `for t`'s produced ∩ consumed set — here that intersection is
    // `{grid}` (the inner stencil loop reads grid[t-1] and writes
    // grid[t] for each t).
    loop t : pipeline=2;

    // The inter-generation edge. async + buffer=2 + notify=event is
    // the M4 capability set; only pthreads-async satisfies it.
    transfer grid : async, buffer=2, notify=event;

    // Fan-in / fan-out hops. `seed` is loaded on host and consumed
    // by `step_or_seed` on compute — genuinely cross-worker, but
    // outside the pipelined loop so a sync transfer is the right
    // shape (the host's `load_input` completes before any iteration
    // begins). `result` is written by the result-stage `ident` loop
    // on host and saved by `save_output` also on host — same-worker,
    // no actual Xfer emitted (`transfer_inject`'s src==dst skip). We
    // still declare both transfers explicitly because PRD §6.3.4
    // forbids implicit defaults.
    transfer seed   : sync;
    transfer result : sync;
}
